  "escalation_active_hydrogen_functionality_check": {
    "conclusion": "Every one of the 29 proposals R-A06 republishes as 'difunctional' (amine_sites==2) is a PRIMARY,PRIMARY diamine with active_nh==4, i.e. TETRAfunctional under the active-hydrogen convention this portfolio uses elsewhere for AHEW/gelation math. Zero of the 38 accepted proposals have active_nh==2. R-A06's corrected 'difunctional' label is therefore built on a nitrogen-atom-count definition that is inconsistent with, and not reconcilable to, the active-hydrogen-based functionality convention used elsewhere in this portfolio and in the field (Lee & Neville 1967; Flory 1953 gelation theory) -- the SAME theory_reference R-A06 itself cites for 'Flory-Stockmayer f'. Under that convention the true difunctional (f=2) yield among these 38 accepted proposals is 0/38, not 29/38.",
    "definition": "AHEW/Flory-Stockmayer convention: functionality = active N-H count (each primary -NH2 reacts twice), NOT nitrogen-atom count. Portfolio precedent: AHEW = 28052.8/amine_value; amine-N-H active-site property receipts.",
    "mda_ddm_known_textbook_case": {
      "active_nh": 4,
      "amine_sites": 2,
      "name": "MDA/DDM(methylene)",
      "note": "MDA/DDM (4,4'-diaminodiphenylmethane) is a well-known TETRAfunctional epoxy curing agent in the published literature (AHEW = MW/4, approx 49.5 g/eq), not difunctional, despite having exactly 2 nitrogen atoms. This is direct external domain evidence that nitrogen-atom count ('amine_sites') is NOT the functionality convention used for epoxy-amine curing stoichiometry/gelation."
    },
